13:06:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That's basically it, yes—though it was messier and more gradual than one heroic volunteer per food item. **Trial and error over generations** Early humans (and pre-human ancestors) tested things by necessity. Someone got hungry enough, tried a plant, and either: - Nothing happened (safe, remember it) - Got sick but survived (maybe edible in small amounts, or only if cooked/processed) - Died or got seriously ill (word spreads, avoid it) This knowledge accumulated over thousands of years and got passed down culturally—through oral tradition, taboos, and food preparation rituals. **Watching animals** Humans also learned by observing what animals ate. If birds or other primates ate a berry safely, that was a decent clue (though not foolproof, since animal digestive systems differ from ours). **Smell, taste, and instinct** Humans (like many animals) have some innate aversion to bitter tastes, which often correlates with toxicity. Rotten smells trigger disgust responses. These aren't perfect systems, but they reduced the odds of poisoning. **Processing techniques as discoveries** Some of the most important "discoveries" weren't just "is this safe" but "how do I make this safe." Examples: - Cassava is toxic raw but safe after soaking/fermenting/cooking - Acorns need leaching to remove tannins - Many beans need cooking to destroy toxins These techniques likely emerged through repeated experimentation—someone left food out longer, or cooked it differently, and noticed it stopped making people sick. **The cost was real** This process absolutely killed and sickened people. Poisoning was a real cause of death throughout human history. Some plants (like certain mushrooms) are still deadly today despite all our accumulated knowledge, which shows how much trial and error was required to map out what's safe. So yes—your instinct is right. It was a slow, sometimes fatal process of experimentation, refined and passed down over countless generations, plus a body already primed with taste-based warning systems (bitterness, disgust at rot) to reduce the death toll along the way.
