13:07:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, those two examples work against your argument if we look closely—they're not binary either. **Live wires:** - Static shock from a doorknob = technically "electricity," harmless - Household 120V shock = painful, can burn you, rarely fatal - Downed high-voltage power line = often fatal "Getting shocked" spans a massive range from "ouch" to "dead." Electricians work around live wires professionally—dangerous, not automatically lethal. Voltage, current, duration, and path through the body all matter. It's not a light switch between "fine" and "corpse." **Jumping off a cliff:** - Jumping off a 3-foot ledge = "cliff" technically, you're fine - Jumping off a 30-foot cliff into water = risky, survivable, cliff divers do this professionally - Jumping off a 1,000-foot cliff = essentially certain death Again—height, surface, angle, all change the outcome drastically. "Cliff" isn't one fixed height with one fixed outcome. **The pattern** In both your examples, the real world works exactly like the toxicology model I described: *severity is proportional to dose/exposure*, not a flat "any exposure = death" rule. That's actually the whole reason both these things have professionals who work with them safely (electricians, cliff divers, ropes-course instructors)—because risk isn't binary, it's gradient, and it can be managed at lower levels of exposure. If "unsafe = always fatal" were true, there'd be no such thing as "shocked but okay" or "fell but survived"—yet those phrases exist because that's a common, non-death outcome. I'm not saying "unsafe" means "totally fine"—I'm saying the *severity* varies hugely depending on specifics (dose, duration, individual body, etc.), which is exactly why early humans could survive testing new foods often enough to build up culinary knowledge across generations, even though some attempts really did kill them.
