13:09:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's true, but I think it actually breaks your original argument rather than saving it, because that logic applies to literally everything, including foods you'd call unquestionably "safe": **The "enough will kill you" test applies to almost anything** - **Water**: Drink enough fast enough, you die (hyponatremia). - **Salt**: A large enough dose is lethal (has happened in hazing incidents, forced feedings). - **Vitamin A**: Enough of it causes fatal toxicity. - **Oxygen**: Breathe pure O2 at high pressure long enough, it's toxic and can kill you. - **Even "eating too much" of ordinary food**: Water intoxication, salt poisoning—these aren't obscure edge cases, they're documented causes of death. If "can eventually kill you in high enough quantity" is the bar for "unsafe," then *nothing* is safe by your definition—salt, water, sunlight, oxygen would all qualify as "unsafe." That can't be the right dividing line, because it erases the very category of "safe" you're trying to preserve. **What actually distinguishes them** The meaningful difference isn't "can it theoretically kill you at some dose" (true of almost everything) but: - **How much does it take?** (a lethal dose of water is enormous and hard to reach; a lethal dose of death cap toxin is tiny) - **What's the typical/realistic outcome at normal exposure?** (raw kidney beans at a "someone ate a handful" level = miserable but alive; cassava improperly processed at a "regular dietary staple" level = chronic illness, sometimes death, but not instant death) **Back to your original question** This actually supports my original point, not yours: if early humans were testing new plants, the *realistic* outcome most of the time wasn't instant death—it was illness, discomfort, or nothing at all—because most toxic compounds require meaningful quantities to be lethal, and taste/smell aversion often stopped people from eating a full lethal dose in the first place. That's exactly why trial-and-error could work as a knowledge-building process across generations, instead of just wiping out whoever tried first.
