13:13:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a common intuition, but I think it's a logical slip worth pointing out—it's similar to saying "the category of dogs is known for barking, so every dog must bark the same amount/loudly." Categories often get named/known for their most notable or extreme members, not their average member. **"Poisonous plants" as a category is huge and varied** It includes: - **Death cap mushroom / hemlock** — tiny doses, often fatal, defines the scary reputation of the category - **Poison ivy** — causes an itchy rash, essentially never fatal, but is 100% correctly called "poisonous" - **Foxglove** — toxic, can affect heart rhythm, dangerous but with huge dose-dependent range from mild symptoms to fatal - **Rhubarb leaves** — oxalic acid, causes illness, fatalities are rare and require large quantities - **Nightshade berries** — toxic, symptoms range from mild GI upset to more severe effects depending on amount eaten All of these are correctly labeled "poisonous plants." But they don't share a uniform lethality just because they're in the same broad bucket. The category is defined by "produces toxins harmful to humans," not by "guaranteed to kill on contact." **Analogy that might land better** "Dangerous animals" is a known category — associated with things like great white sharks and grizzly bears (which can absolutely kill you). But the same category also includes stinging jellyfish (rarely fatal), venomous spiders (many bites cause pain/swelling, not death), and aggressive geese (annoying, not lethal). We don't say "since dangerous animals are known for killing people, every dangerous animal must be equally lethal." **Why the category still deserves respect** None of this means "don't worry about poisonous plants" — quite the opposite, it means you can't predict from the label alone how dangerous a specific plant is, which is exactly why caution/avoidance is the smart default rather than trying to guess where something falls on the spectrum.
