13:15:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a clever angle, but I think the logic doesn't actually get you to "unsafe = deadly." If anything, it points the other way. **Evolution optimizes for "avoid it," not "predict exact lethality"** The disgust response isn't a precise fatality detector—it's a *blunt* avoidance mechanism. Evolutionarily, a false alarm (avoiding something that's actually harmless or just mildly bad) costs you almost nothing. But a missed warning (eating something genuinely lethal) could cost you your life. So natural selection favors an oversensitive system that fires on lots of things—bitter, sour, rotten-smelling—regardless of whether the specific compound is "will make you queasy" or "will kill you outright." It's not calibrated to lethality specifically; it's calibrated to "better safe than sorry" broadly. That's actually why so many bitter compounds in nature are only mildly toxic (many bitter greens, unripe fruits) rather than fatal—the system flags them the same way it flags truly deadly stuff, because fine-tuned precision wasn't the evolutionary priority. A warning system tuned to catch every lethal threat necessarily also flags a bunch of non-lethal ones as collateral, since the whole strategy is "cast a wide net." **So the existence of the disgust response actually supports the gradient view** If unsafe always equaled deadly, you'd expect the aversion system to be much more selective—firing only on the truly fatal stuff. Instead, it fires broadly and imprecisely on anything bitter/rotten/off, which lines up much better with "wide range of harm, use caution as a blanket rule" than with "this system only exists because everything unsafe kills you."
